{"hierarchy":{"paths":[["doc:\/\/Covering\/documentation\/Covering","doc:\/\/Covering\/documentation\/Covering\/GraphProtocols","doc:\/\/Covering\/documentation\/Covering\/GraphProtocols\/Graph"]]},"kind":"symbol","identifier":{"url":"doc:\/\/Covering\/documentation\/Covering\/GraphProtocols\/Graph\/minimumDominatingSet()","interfaceLanguage":"swift"},"sections":[],"abstract":[{"text":"A minimum dominating set (exact): every vertex is in it or adjacent to one in it, and of","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"the smallest such sets it is the lexicographically least by vertex index (the rule of","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"code":"maximumIndependentSet()","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":"), in ","type":"text"},{"code":"vertices","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" order; [] for the empty graph. Self-loops and","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"parallel edges do not matter; isolated vertices are in it.","type":"text"}],"variants":[{"traits":[{"interfaceLanguage":"swift"}],"paths":["\/documentation\/covering\/graphprotocols\/graph\/minimumdominatingset()"]}],"primaryContentSections":[{"declarations":[{"platforms":["Linux"],"languages":["swift"],"tokens":[{"text":"func","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"minimumDominatingSet","kind":"identifier"},{"text":"() -> [","kind":"text"},{"text":"Self","kind":"typeIdentifier"},{"text":".","kind":"text"},{"preciseIdentifier":"s:14GraphProtocols0A0P6VertexQa","text":"Vertex","kind":"typeIdentifier","identifier":"doc:\/\/GraphProtocols\/documentation\/GraphProtocols\/Graph\/Vertex"},{"text":"]","kind":"text"}]}],"kind":"declarations"},{"content":[{"text":"Discussion","type":"heading","anchor":"discussion","level":2},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Each connected component is solved alone; a vertex adjacent to all the others of its"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"component answers alone. Otherwise branch and reduce: after every choice, an undominated"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"vertex with one possible dominator gets it, and a vertex whose undominated closed"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"neighbourhood lies in another’s is ruled out (for the lexicographic pass only in favour of"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"a lesser vertex, which keeps the least set; this leaf rule settles paths and many trees"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"outright). The search branches on the most-coverage dominator of an undominated vertex"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"with the fewest left and prunes on the budget, on the largest coverages, and on a greedy"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"2-packing (vertices with no dominator in common, each needing its own; on trees the largest"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"2-packing is γ, Meir and Moon 1975). γ comes from a greedy descent and decisions one"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"smaller; the least set of γ vertices is then decided vertex by vertex in index order"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"against a witness, each decision searching only the part of the problem the vertex"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"touches. Sparse components keep O(n + m) words of state; dense ones (mean degree d with d²"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"above c ⌈c \/ 64⌉) use bitset closed neighbourhoods, under 2m words. Exponential in the"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"component size in the worst case."}],"type":"paragraph"}],"kind":"content"}],"schemaVersion":{"patch":0,"minor":3,"major":0},"metadata":{"title":"minimumDominatingSet()","modules":[{"relatedModules":["GraphProtocols"],"name":"Covering"}],"symbolKind":"method","externalID":"s:14GraphProtocols0A0P8CoveringE20minimumDominatingSetSay6VertexQzGyF","fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"func"},{"kind":"text","text":